const TERMS:LegalPageItem[]=[{"id":"p1","title":"Agreement and scope","summary":"What these Terms cover","bodyHtml":"<p>These Terms govern access to Cookie, including the Cookie website, web application, installed web experience, account features, chat, file handling, research features, projects, Memory, sharing, Code Studio, and other features that link to these Terms. A feature can have additional instructions shown in the product; those instructions supplement these Terms rather than silently changing them. Cookie is a service, not a promise that a particular model, interface, integration, or capability will remain available forever.</p><p>Using Cookie means you are responsible for complying with these Terms and with laws that apply to your use. You accept these Terms when you create an account, sign in, or otherwise use a part of Cookie that is governed by them. You should read the <a href=\"#privacy\" data-legal-kind=\"privacy\">Privacy Policy</a> as well because it explains personal-information processing and is a separate document from these Terms. The two documents should be read together when you are deciding what information to submit and how you plan to use the service.</p><p>These Terms are written for the actual Cookie product rather than for a generic software business. They therefore describe practical features that exist in Cookie today, while intentionally using qualified wording such as “may,” “where available,” or “where enabled” for features that can change by account, device, plan, rollout, or release. A feature appearing in this document does not create a contractual promise that the feature will always be offered.</p><p>Examples help explain scope. A normal chat, a conversation saved to history, a file sent through the composer, a project file, a Memory item, a shared chat, a Code Studio operation, or a support-related account action can each involve different product systems. These Terms apply across those systems unless a more specific service term displayed to you says otherwise. Product messages and account notices can add operational requirements, such as limits or security checks.</p><p>Nothing in these Terms is intended to misstate the legal relationship between you and the operator of Cookie or to waive rights that applicable law does not allow to be waived. If a local rule gives you a mandatory consumer, privacy, contract, or other right, that rule continues to apply to the extent required. Any dispute about the meaning or enforceability of a particular clause should be resolved using applicable law and the facts of the actual service relationship.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Agreement and scope” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p2","title":"Accounts and eligibility","summary":"Creating and maintaining an account","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie uses user accounts to keep identity, sign-in state, plan information, credits, saved conversations, projects, Memory, and other account-level data associated with the correct person. When you register, you are expected to provide information that is accurate enough for the account to function. You must not impersonate another person or intentionally create misleading account information for fraud, evasion, harassment, or unauthorized access.</p><p>You are responsible for activity performed through your account unless the activity resulted from Cookie's own error or unauthorized access that you could not reasonably prevent. Keep the email account connected to Cookie accessible because Cookie can use email for account verification and secure password-reset links. Do not give another person your password, verification code, reset link, or active session access. Sharing credentials can make it difficult to distinguish your activity from another person's activity.</p><p>Cookie does not currently require you to invent or submit a separate government identity record simply to use the ordinary account flow. That does not mean identity questions can never arise. Cookie may ask for additional verification during security, abuse, recovery, or account-support processes when necessary to protect the account or the service. You should not send unnecessary identity documents through chat or file upload just to prove ownership when a normal account control can resolve the issue.</p><p>Eligibility can depend on the laws that apply where you live. Cookie does not use these Terms to invent a universal age rule that is not implemented in the product. You are responsible for making sure you are legally permitted to use the service, and a parent or guardian should supervise use by a minor where local law or household rules require it. If Cookie later introduces an age-gating or age-verification feature, that product control can add requirements to this general eligibility rule.</p><p>You can stop using Cookie at any time, subject to any outstanding obligations that by their nature continue after use ends. Account closure does not necessarily mean every technical or legally relevant record disappears instantly; some records can be kept for security, fraud prevention, moderation, dispute handling, or operational reasons described in the <a href=\"#privacy\" data-legal-kind=\"privacy\">Privacy Policy</a>. Account access can also be limited temporarily while an identity, security, or moderation issue is being reviewed.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Accounts and eligibility” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p3","title":"Authentication and account security","summary":"Passwords, sessions, verification and recovery","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie's current account system can use email verification, password authentication, secure password-reset links, and session cookies. Password-reset emails are designed to provide a time-limited link rather than exposing the password to an administrator. Treat the account email address and reset flow as security controls. Anyone who gets access to your email inbox or an active session may be able to use the account until access is revoked, so protect those systems too.</p><p>You must not attempt to obtain another user's session, verification code, password, reset token, or account recovery information. You must not test authentication boundaries against production accounts, replay security tokens, or exploit weaknesses for the purpose of accessing data that is not yours. Responsible security research should avoid accessing real user content and should not interrupt availability. Report a suspected security problem through the support route made available by Cookie.</p><p>Cookie can invalidate sessions after certain account actions, including moderation actions and password-reset activity. This is a security behavior, not a guarantee that every compromised device or browser is immediately harmless. You remain responsible for checking devices you no longer trust and changing passwords through the normal reset process when needed. A password reset link should be treated as a secret until used or expired.</p><p>Repeated failed authentication or suspicious activity may result in a temporary lock, extra verification, or other protection. Such a protection can be triggered without any finding that the account owner intentionally did something wrong; its purpose is to reduce abuse and unauthorized access. Support may need enough information to distinguish an account owner from someone attempting to take over the account.</p><p>Security controls can evolve. Cookie may change session lifetimes, authentication providers, rate limits, recovery requirements, or detection methods when necessary to protect accounts. Product documentation or an on-screen notice can explain a new control. Nothing here authorizes a user to disable or bypass a security measure.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Authentication and account security” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p4","title":"Acceptable use","summary":"Using Cookie lawfully and responsibly","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie is an AI productivity service. You can use it for ordinary conversations, writing, study, analysis, research, coding, project work, document analysis, image-related tasks, and other lawful purposes supported by the product. Your responsibility is not merely to avoid obvious crime; you must also avoid using the service to violate another person's rights, expose private information without authorization, or interfere with the service for others.</p><p>Do not use Cookie to commit fraud, impersonate people for deception, conduct credential theft, distribute malware, launch unauthorized attacks, facilitate theft, extortion, stalking, targeted harassment, or other harmful activity. Do not use it to evade security controls or to create instructions specifically intended to compromise accounts, networks, devices, or the Cookie platform. A technical capability that can generate code or text does not turn an otherwise prohibited act into an allowed one.</p><p>Do not use Cookie as a replacement for professional judgment in situations where an error can seriously harm a person or organization. Medical, legal, financial, employment, safety, and other high-impact decisions can require qualified professionals and primary sources. You may ask Cookie to help organize information or prepare questions, but you remain responsible for the real-world decision and for checking important statements before relying on them.</p><p>You must respect the rights of third parties. Upload or share only information, files, images, code, or other material that you are authorized to use. Do not use Cookie to reveal private information about another person merely because the system is technically capable of transforming or summarizing it. Do not ask Cookie to evade copyright, access controls, privacy restrictions, or confidentiality duties.</p><p>These rules apply whether the activity happens in a chat, project, file upload, shared link, Code Studio, or another Cookie surface. Attempted circumvention is treated as part of the conduct as well. Cookie may refuse a request, limit access, or take account-level action when a use pattern presents a credible safety, security, legal, or abuse problem.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Acceptable use” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p5","title":"Prohibited technical activity","summary":"Security, abuse, circumvention and interference","bodyHtml":"<p>You may not probe Cookie infrastructure, reverse engineer protected server behavior, bypass authentication, defeat rate limits, or attempt to obtain another user's content. You may not intentionally send traffic designed to exhaust service capacity or make the product unavailable to others. You may not insert malicious payloads into prompts, project files, uploaded documents, or code in order to exploit Cookie or a person who later handles the content.</p><p>Cookie has multiple moving parts, including the web client, server-side functions, databases, authentication, AI model calls, email delivery, and security controls. An endpoint being reachable from a browser does not mean that it is authorized for arbitrary use. Do not assume that an API route, network response, object identifier, or client-side value is permission to access or alter another user's data. Access decisions are made by the service, not by whatever a browser happens to expose.</p><p>Rate limits and account limits are part of normal service operation. You may not rotate accounts, automate registrations, alter client behavior, or otherwise construct a workflow whose purpose is to evade a limit that applies to your account. You may not manipulate credits, plan state, model routing, moderation state, or other account metadata through unsupported requests or direct database access.</p><p>Legitimate interoperability can be different from circumvention. Where applicable law gives you a right that cannot be removed by contract, that right remains in force. Otherwise, use the interfaces and documented product controls Cookie provides. A request that causes load, security risk, or privacy exposure can be restricted even when the same general goal can be accomplished safely through supported features.</p><p>If Cookie detects behavior consistent with compromise, abuse, fraud, or deliberate circumvention, it may protect the platform by limiting sessions, changing access, suspending an account, or taking another proportionate action. Enforcement records can be retained so that repeated behavior is not treated as an isolated event.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Prohibited technical activity” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p6","title":"Your inputs","summary":"Prompts, messages, documents, images and code","bodyHtml":"<p>You retain whatever rights you already have in material you submit to Cookie, subject to the rights of others and applicable law. You are responsible for having the necessary permission to submit prompts, documents, images, source code, personal information, or other material. If you are working for an employer, school, client, or other organization, check whether that organization restricts what you may upload to external services.</p><p>Submitting material gives Cookie the operational permission needed to process that material so the feature you requested can work. For example, a file may need to be transmitted to the service, stored with a conversation or project, parsed into text, or passed to an AI model or related provider. This is a functional processing permission, not a transfer of ownership of your underlying material.</p><p>Do not submit secrets merely because the interface accepts text. Passwords, private keys, authentication tokens, confidential business information, financial account credentials, or highly sensitive personal information can be risky to place in any online service. A “private chat” label is not the same thing as an absolute secrecy guarantee. Use the minimum information necessary for the task.</p><p>When you provide content that belongs to someone else, you are responsible for the permission required to use it. This includes copyrighted material, confidential work product, private communications, customer data, and data covered by contracts. Cookie does not verify ownership for every upload and cannot turn unauthorized access into authorized use.</p><p>You also remain responsible for the way you use uploaded information after Cookie processes it. If you download, copy, publish, share, or place the resulting material into another system, your obligations continue. Review generated summaries and transformations for missing context, privacy leakage, or inaccurate interpretation before forwarding them.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Your inputs” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p7","title":"Outputs and AI accuracy","summary":"Generated text, code, images, summaries and decisions","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie's responses are generated by AI systems. Output can contain factual errors, omissions, stale information, incorrect calculations, unsupported assumptions, or wording that sounds confident even when it is wrong. A fluent response is not evidence that the underlying claim has been independently verified. Similar prompts can also produce different results, and different users can receive similar or overlapping output.</p><p>When Cookie provides research or source links, those sources can help you verify a response, but the presence of a source does not guarantee that the generated interpretation is correct. Read the original material when the detail matters. For calculations, check inputs and units. For code, review dependencies, permissions, data handling, tests, and deployment assumptions. For generated images or files, inspect the actual output rather than assuming the description exactly matches it.</p><p>You are responsible for how you use Output. Cookie does not decide whether a generated answer is appropriate for a particular customer, classroom, workplace, legal filing, financial decision, production system, or safety-critical process. You should add human review where the consequences of error justify it. The more consequential the task, the less appropriate it is to rely on a single model response without independent checking.</p><p>Output may include content you did not expect or that you disagree with. Cookie's generation process can reproduce common patterns in training data or the current conversation, and it can make mistakes in tone or factual interpretation. You may request a revision, correction, alternative approach, or explanation, but a revised response is still generated content and should be evaluated on its merits.</p><p>Cookie does not promise that every model result is original, unique, complete, or fit for a particular purpose. Nothing in these Terms changes the ownership or license rights you may have under applicable law. When a third party's material is included in an output, your rights to that material can be limited by that third party's rights.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Outputs and AI accuracy” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p8","title":"Files and images","summary":"Uploads, processing and generated visual content","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie can accept supported files and images through its interface for analysis or other tasks. When you upload a file, the service may inspect its metadata, extract text, create an internal representation, or send relevant content to an AI processing system. Supported file types, size limits, and feature availability can change. Do not upload a file simply because the interface permits it; upload it because you have a legitimate reason and the necessary rights.</p><p>Images can be used as source material for analysis and, where enabled, Cookie can generate images. Generated images can contain defects, misread text, visual artifacts, or content that does not exactly match a request. You should review generated visual material before using it in a commercial, public, educational, or professional context. A visual result does not establish that every depicted person, logo, object, or location is accurately represented.</p><p>Uploaded files can remain associated with the chat or project where you used them according to that feature's behavior. Deleting a visible chat does not guarantee that every technical copy has already disappeared from every intermediate system. Some records can be retained for security, abuse prevention, legal obligations, or operational recovery as described in the <a href=\"#privacy\" data-legal-kind=\"privacy\">Privacy Policy</a>.</p><p>You must not upload malware, stolen credential databases, confidential records that you are not authorized to disclose, or material intended to attack the service or another person. A file can carry active content or hidden data even when its visible text looks harmless. You are responsible for scanning and handling your own files appropriately before and after using Cookie.</p><p>When you download or export a generated file, the responsibility shifts to how you store and use that copy. Cookie cannot control where you send it next. Treat generated documents and images as normal digital files: protect confidential material, check accuracy, and remove data you do not have a legitimate reason to retain.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Files and images” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p9","title":"Search and research","summary":"Live web information, sources and citations","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie can decide to search the web when a request benefits from current information. Search can be useful for recent facts, documentation, comparisons, public pages, and research questions, but a search result is not automatically authoritative. Websites can be incomplete, biased, inaccessible, outdated, copied from one another, or changed after a response is generated.</p><p>When a response provides source links or citations, use them as a starting point for verification. Check who published the material, when it was updated, what evidence it cites, and whether the source actually supports the claim being made. Cookie can summarize a source incorrectly even when the underlying source is legitimate. A citation proves that a source was referenced; it does not prove that the generated conclusion is correct.</p><p>Search can involve third-party websites that Cookie does not control. Their own terms, privacy notices, paywalls, cookies, and technical restrictions can apply when you follow a link. Do not assume that because Cookie can read a public page, you are permitted to scrape, republish, or redistribute everything on that page.</p><p>For high-stakes research, prefer primary documents and authoritative sources. Cookie can help compare material, identify unanswered questions, or prepare a research plan, but you remain responsible for confirming critical details. Current information can also change between the time a source is retrieved and the time you act on it.</p><p>Cookie may be unable to fetch a page, may encounter a robots or network restriction, or may receive an incomplete response. In those cases it should not be represented as having verified information that it did not successfully retrieve. You should treat a missing source or failed search as uncertainty rather than evidence that the opposite claim is true.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Search and research” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p10","title":"Code Studio","summary":"Code, repositories, files and engineering actions","bodyHtml":"<p>Code Studio can be used to inspect and modify project files within the boundaries of the Cookie product. Generated code is still generated code: it can contain security flaws, incorrect assumptions, missing dependencies, type errors, or behavior that differs from your intended application. Review important changes and test them before deploying to production or giving them to another team.</p><p>You authorize Cookie to perform actions that you explicitly request through supported Code Studio controls. The service can read project files, create or update files, manage revisions, and run checks where the product exposes those operations. Protected areas and security rules can restrict some operations. A user request does not override a protected-file rule or another server-side permission check.</p><p>You are responsible for the repository or project content you bring into Code Studio and for ensuring that it does not contain secrets or material you are not permitted to process. Do not assume that a private codebase stays private if you intentionally share it through a third-party integration or public link. Review generated configuration changes carefully because a small edit can affect authentication, billing, network access, or data retention.</p><p>When Code Studio reports that a build, review, check, or deployment-related step succeeded, use the actual result for confirmation. Cookie should not claim that a build or deployment succeeded when it does not have supporting tool output. Similarly, you should not treat an AI-generated explanation as a substitute for checking the real repository state, tests, logs, and deployed application.</p><p>The safe way to use Code Studio is iterative: state the intended change, let the service inspect relevant files, review the proposed or applied changes, run validation, and verify the resulting application. Nothing in these Terms requires you to accept code just because Cookie generated it, and nothing transfers your obligations as an owner or operator of the underlying project.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Code Studio” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p11","title":"Memory, Projects and saved work","summary":"Persistent workspace features","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie can provide Memory and workspace features that help keep useful information available across interactions. Memory is distinct from an individual chat message: when Memory is enabled, selected information can be stored as a separate saved item. Projects can group related work, and saved items can preserve material you intentionally save. These features are optional product mechanisms, not a promise that Cookie will remember every detail.</p><p>You decide what information is appropriate to keep in Memory or a workspace. Do not save passwords, verification codes, private keys, recovery phrases, or other secrets in Memory. Even ordinary preferences can be sensitive in context. Review saved information periodically and delete items that are no longer useful. Removing a saved Memory item does not necessarily erase the same information from every chat, project file, export, or other record where it also appears.</p><p>Projects can contain conversations, files, descriptions, or other work associated with you. The exact project controls can evolve, but the basic rule is consistent: treat a project as an organized workspace, not as an isolated security boundary from every other system you use. Anyone who receives a shared link or who you otherwise authorize to access a piece of work may be able to see the information included in that share.</p><p>Saved work can be copied, downloaded, edited, or reused. You remain responsible for the correctness and rights associated with the material you choose to preserve. Cookie does not verify that a saved document is free from confidential information, intellectual-property restrictions, or malware. Before sharing a project result, check both its content and its audience.</p><p>Because workspace features can change, product controls shown in Cookie are the best source for the exact current behavior of a Memory or project action. These Terms explain the underlying responsibility: you choose what to save, you should protect sensitive information, and you should not assume that a saved item is automatically permanent, private forever, or deleted from every technical system the instant a button is pressed.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Memory, Projects and saved work” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p12","title":"Sharing and public links","summary":"What happens when you share work","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie can provide a sharing feature that creates a view of selected conversation content. A shared link is different from a private chat because a person who receives or discovers the link may be able to read the material included in the share. You are responsible for reviewing the conversation before creating a share, especially when it contains names, customer information, private correspondence, screenshots, credentials, or other sensitive material.</p><p>Sharing is an intentional publication action. Do not assume that changing the title of a chat removes private information from the message content. Read through the material that will be exposed, check attachments, and consider whether the recipient should receive the whole conversation or only a smaller excerpt. A link can also be copied by a recipient and circulated beyond the audience you originally had in mind.</p><p>When Cookie provides a control to remove or disable a share, using that control can prevent normal access through that shared item. It does not give you control over screenshots, downloaded copies, quotes, or other copies that someone already made before the share was disabled. This is why the safest rule is to avoid sharing information that you would not want recipients to retain.</p><p>You must have the right to share the material. The fact that you wrote a prompt does not automatically give you the right to publish information supplied by someone else. Confidentiality agreements, privacy duties, copyright, employment rules, school policies, and other obligations can still apply.</p><p>Public or link-accessible content can also be indexed, copied, or referenced by systems outside Cookie, depending on the recipient's actions. Cookie does not promise to locate and erase every downstream copy. Treat a share link as a publication mechanism and use the least amount of information necessary for the intended audience.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Sharing and public links” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p13","title":"Plans, credits and usage limits","summary":"Free and paid access, model access and credits","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie can offer different plans, model access levels, AI limits, and credits. The features visible to you depend on the plan and account state actually recorded by Cookie. A plan label does not guarantee unrestricted use of every model or capability. Product limits can exist to manage capacity, protect the service, or provide different levels of access.</p><p>Credits are account-level usage units when Cookie displays them. They are not a bank account, security deposit, or general-purpose currency unless Cookie explicitly says so in a current purchase flow. An administrator can grant or adjust credits through authorized controls, and promotional or gifted credits can have conditions shown when they are issued. Do not attempt to change the displayed balance through client-side manipulation or unsupported API calls.</p><p>Usage limits can apply to messages, images, models, or other capabilities. A limit can be enforced before a request runs, during processing, or after a usage event is recorded. The exact accounting method can change as the system evolves. When a request is denied because of a limit, that does not mean the account is broken; it can simply mean the current allowance has been reached.</p><p>If Cookie offers a paid purchase, the checkout or purchase interface is the source of the price and transaction-specific terms shown at the time you buy. Cookie should not be described as charging a user a price that is not actually shown to that user. Taxes, payment-provider terms, refunds, renewals, or consumer rights can depend on the transaction and applicable law.</p><p>Do not resell account access, credits, or plan privileges unless Cookie explicitly provides a feature that permits it. Do not move credits between accounts, create accounts solely to evade limits, or falsely represent a Free, Pro, or MAX entitlement. Plan and credit state is controlled by the service database and authorized administrative actions, not by the client interface.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Plans, credits and usage limits” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p14","title":"Payments and transaction terms","summary":"Purchases, renewals, refunds and billing records","bodyHtml":"<p>If Cookie introduces or maintains paid access, the amount you are asked to pay is the amount displayed in the applicable purchase flow, subject to any taxes, fees, discounts, or transaction terms shown there. Cookie should not rely on hidden pricing. Before confirming a purchase, review the plan name, duration, amount, renewal behavior, and any refund or cancellation information presented to you.</p><p>A transaction can create account records needed to recognize your plan or entitlement. Those records can include the plan state, entitlement dates, payment-related identifiers supplied by a payment system, or administrative adjustments. Cookie's current account schema records plan and expiration information; the presence of a plan field does not by itself prove that a specific external payment succeeded.</p><p>Refunds and cancellations are handled according to the purchase terms actually applicable to the transaction and any mandatory rights under law. This document intentionally does not invent a universal refund period because the correct rule can depend on the transaction method, user location, product configuration, and the terms shown at purchase time. Contact support with the relevant account and transaction information if a billing problem needs investigation.</p><p>Do not send full card numbers, card security codes, bank passwords, or payment credentials through a normal Cookie chat to request billing help. Provide only the information the support process asks for. Cookie administrators can change plan state or credits through authorized internal controls, but that internal ability does not mean a user should submit financial secrets to an administrator.</p><p>Billing information can also be subject to the privacy terms and to the terms of a payment provider, if one is used. Keep your purchase confirmations and transaction records if you may need to dispute, reconcile, or cancel a transaction later.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Payments and transaction terms” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p15","title":"Third-party services","summary":"External infrastructure, providers and linked websites","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie relies on external infrastructure to operate parts of the service. The current application code uses Neon-backed database storage, Cloudflare Pages/Functions infrastructure, Cloudflare Workers AI capabilities, and an SMTP-based email path for verification, password-reset, and release-announcement messages. These providers are operational components of the service, not additional Cookie accounts created for you.</p><p>When a feature requires a third-party system, information can be processed by that provider to the extent needed to deliver the feature. The exact data flow depends on the feature. For example, a chat request can require model processing; account authentication can require database access; a password-reset request can require email delivery; and a website request is necessarily handled by the web infrastructure serving Cookie.</p><p>Third-party providers can have their own service terms, acceptable-use rules, privacy notices, operational limitations, and security practices. Cookie is responsible for configuring and using its integrations, but it does not control every independent action of an external provider. You should read the provider's terms when a feature sends you to or directly uses an external site.</p><p>Cookie can also link to public sources during web research. A source website is not necessarily a provider acting on Cookie's behalf; it can simply be a separate website that publishes information. When you visit an external link, its own policies can apply.</p><p>The list of providers and the technical architecture can change over time. These Terms describe the components that are actually used by the current Cookie application and avoid claiming that a provider is used for a purpose that the product does not currently perform. Material changes to the service can be reflected in updated documentation or notices.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Third-party services” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p16","title":"Privacy and data relationship","summary":"How these Terms connect to the Privacy Policy","bodyHtml":"<p>The <a href=\"#privacy\" data-legal-kind=\"privacy\">Privacy Policy</a> explains how Cookie processes personal information. These Terms explain the rules for using the service. A user should read both before submitting information that may be sensitive, confidential, regulated, or difficult to remove later. The fact that data is described in the <a href=\"#privacy\" data-legal-kind=\"privacy\">Privacy Policy</a> does not give a user permission to upload information they were not authorized to disclose.</p><p>Cookie can process account data, conversation content, uploaded files, project information, Memory items, usage events, session information, moderation records, and other technical data when necessary to operate and protect the service. This processing is governed by the detailed descriptions in the <a href=\"#privacy\" data-legal-kind=\"privacy\">Privacy Policy</a>, not by a general assumption that every piece of information is collected or used in every way.</p><p>You should use the minimum amount of personal information necessary for the task. When a task can be completed with a synthetic example, placeholder, redacted document, or less identifying detail, using that approach can reduce privacy risk. Cookie does not promise that a chat is the same as an offline notebook or that a user can prevent all server-side processing simply by choosing a temporary or private-looking interface.</p><p>If you intentionally share a conversation or project, the privacy situation changes because the audience can expand beyond the original account. You should therefore treat sharing as a new disclosure. The <a href=\"#privacy\" data-legal-kind=\"privacy\">Privacy Policy</a> explains how Cookie processes the shared information, while these Terms explain that you are responsible for choosing whether and how to publish it.</p><p>Nothing in these Terms promises compliance with a specific law, certification, or regulatory program unless Cookie expressly states that status in a current official document. Legal obligations can depend on location and context. The correct way to understand your privacy rights is to read the current <a href=\"#privacy\" data-legal-kind=\"privacy\">Privacy Policy</a> and use the privacy or support contacts it provides.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Privacy and data relationship” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p17","title":"Safety, moderation and enforcement","summary":"Warnings, suspensions, bans and review","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie can use moderation and security controls to protect users, the service, and the reliability of the platform. Current account administration can record warning, suspension, ban, unsuspension, unban, and related moderation events. A moderation event can include an action, a reason, a duration where relevant, and a timestamp. Users can receive in-product notifications about moderation activity and, for some restrictions, login can show the reason and suspension end time.</p><p>A warning is an account-level enforcement action that can record a moderation note. A suspension can temporarily block normal account access and can include an end time. A ban can block access until an authorized administrator restores the account. These descriptions explain Cookie's current account-state model; they do not promise that every warning will have an automatic expiration timer or that every case will follow the same workflow.</p><p>Cookie can consider the nature of the behavior, security risk, repeated conduct, impact on others, and applicable rules when deciding whether to take action. It can also act on security concerns or legal requirements even when an issue is not simply a content-policy violation. Administrative actions are subject to role permissions inside Cookie, with higher-risk controls reserved for authorized roles.</p><p>Enforcement does not require Cookie to publish private detection logic or every piece of evidence used to protect the service. Revealing internal security rules can make them easier to defeat. At the same time, users should receive the account information Cookie is designed to provide, such as an enforcement reason or temporary end time when one is available. Appeals or support review can be used when Cookie provides that route.</p><p>Trying to evade a suspension or ban by creating another account, using someone else's account, or manipulating client state is not an acceptable response. Such evasion can create additional security or moderation events and can make restoration harder. The safest path is to use the review or support mechanism available through Cookie.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Safety, moderation and enforcement” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p18","title":"Availability, changes and beta features","summary":"Service evolution and operational limits","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie is continuously developed. Models, features, interfaces, infrastructure, limits, and supporting providers can change. A feature that exists today can be redesigned, temporarily disabled, replaced, or removed. This is especially true for experimental or beta features. These Terms describe the service at a level that remains useful while the product evolves, but the current interface and Help Center remain the best source for exact feature behavior.</p><p>Service availability can be affected by maintenance, deployments, provider incidents, network problems, capacity limits, model availability, security events, or other technical conditions. Cookie does not promise uninterrupted service every minute of every day. When a service component fails, the correct status is based on the actual system result rather than on an assumption that every request will always succeed.</p><p>Cookie can change implementation details without changing the fundamental purpose of the service. For example, the model selected for a task, the way search is routed, the available limits, or the technical provider behind an email or database operation can be updated. Product documentation may describe the visible result without exposing internal architecture that would create security risk.</p><p>Some features can be offered experimentally. Experimental behavior can have tighter limits, more defects, incomplete documentation, or shorter notice before changes. You should not use a beta feature as the sole foundation for a safety-critical or legally significant process unless you have independently validated it and accepted the operational risk.</p><p>When a change materially affects an account entitlement or requires user action, Cookie can provide notice through the product or an account contact method where appropriate. These Terms do not promise a specific notice period because the correct approach can depend on the change, urgency, and applicable law.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Availability, changes and beta features” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p19","title":"Intellectual property and feedback","summary":"Cookie software, branding, user rights and suggestions","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie's software, interface design, service branding, logos, visual assets, and underlying technology are protected by applicable intellectual-property laws. Except as expressly permitted by these Terms or applicable law, you may not copy, sell, lease, redistribute, or create a competing service from Cookie's protected implementation. Normal use of the product does not transfer ownership of Cookie's code or trademarks to the user.</p><p>You retain rights in your own Input subject to rights held by others. Your use of Output is also subject to applicable law, third-party rights, and the limitations described in the Output section of these Terms. Because AI output can resemble material produced for another user, Cookie does not promise that every output is unique or that receiving a particular output gives you exclusive rights over every similar result.</p><p>If you send feedback about the product, such as a bug report, feature idea, interface suggestion, or usability observation, Cookie may use that feedback to operate or improve the service without owing you a separate payment for the idea itself. Do not include confidential secrets in feedback merely to explain a suggestion. Use a minimal reproducible example when possible.</p><p>“Cookie” and related branding identify the service and should not be used in a way that falsely suggests sponsorship, endorsement, employment, or partnership. Third-party names and marks remain owned by their respective holders. If a generated image, document, or code sample contains a third-party mark, your later commercial or public use can still be restricted by that third party's rights.</p><p>You are responsible for respecting intellectual-property rules when uploading, generating, downloading, or publishing material. Cookie cannot inspect every license or determine whether a real-world use is fair, permitted, or exempt. When the rights question matters, consult the actual license or qualified legal advice rather than treating a model answer as the final determination.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The service-level rule for “Intellectual property and feedback” is practical: use the controls Cookie actually provides, do not infer permissions from a client-side display, and do not rely on an AI response as evidence that an action is authorized. A feature can touch several systems at once, so the rule should be applied together with the sections covering accounts, content, security, privacy, moderation, and service availability. Where the product shows a specific limit, notice, confirmation, or restriction, that current product behavior is the operational instruction to follow.</p><p>For example, a user should check the account state before assuming an action succeeded, review a generated result before publishing it, and keep a copy of important work outside Cookie when loss would matter. Cookie may change implementation details behind the interface, but it should not be read as granting a right merely because a browser can display a request or because an AI model can describe how to perform it. The safe interpretation of this section is therefore narrow: use Cookie for legitimate purposes, follow the product controls, and ask support when the service state is unclear.</p>"},{"id":"p20","title":"Account ending, liability and disputes","summary":"Termination, risk limits, disputes and how to contact Cookie","bodyHtml":"<p>Termination and account restoration are covered here together with the service's risk allocation and practical dispute path.</p><h3>Account ending and continuing duties</h3><p>Cookie can restrict, suspend, or terminate account access when reasonably necessary to address violations of these Terms, security incidents, fraud, abuse, payment problems where applicable, or legal obligations. The exact action can depend on severity, repetition, risk, and the account state recorded by the service. A restriction can be temporary or can remain until an authorized administrator reviews the account.</p><p>Temporary suspension can be associated with an end time. A suspension ending does not necessarily erase the underlying moderation event, because the event can be needed for security, abuse prevention, and audit purposes. Similarly, unbanning an account restores the account state but does not imply that historical enforcement records are deleted.</p><p>You may stop using or close an account through the controls Cookie provides. Where a direct deletion function is not available, contact the support route shown by Cookie. Closing an account should not be understood as a command to erase all records instantly from backups, security logs, moderation systems, or legal records that are legitimately retained.</p><p>Restrictions can also arise without misconduct. For example, a security system can temporarily lock an account after unusual authentication attempts. Such a lock can be protective rather than punitive. You should follow the recovery instructions instead of trying to work around the control.</p><p>Clauses that by their nature must continue after termination can continue, including rules concerning past conduct, intellectual property, disclaimers, liability limits, and records that Cookie is permitted or required to retain. Termination does not give either side a right to rewrite what happened before the account ended.</p><p>Cookie is provided on an “as available” and “as is” basis to the maximum extent permitted by applicable law. The service can experience bugs, outages, delayed responses, provider failures, inaccurate model output, broken links, network errors, incompatible files, or changes in available capabilities. You should maintain backups and independent copies of important work instead of treating Cookie as your only repository.</p><p>Cookie does not promise that every response will be correct, current, complete, secure, uninterrupted, or suitable for your intended purpose. This includes generated text, code, images, research summaries, calculations, project edits, or other Output. You remain responsible for deciding when a result is safe and appropriate to use.</p><p>To the maximum extent permitted by applicable law, Cookie and its operators are not responsible for indirect, incidental, special, consequential, exemplary, or punitive losses arising from or related to use of the service. This can include losses connected with interruption, loss of opportunity, loss of data, or reliance on an incorrect AI result, subject to any liability that the law does not permit the parties to exclude.</p><p>Nothing in these Terms is intended to exclude liability for matters that applicable law makes non-excludable, or to remove mandatory rights you have under consumer or other laws that apply to your relationship with Cookie. Where a local rule imposes a different standard, that rule controls to the required extent.</p><p>You should treat these limits as practical risk guidance. Do not place irreplaceable data only in Cookie, do not give generated code production authority without review, do not publish a share link containing information you could not safely disclose, and do not make a high-impact decision solely because Cookie produced a confident response.</p><p>If something goes wrong, the first step is to use the support contact or account-support route that Cookie makes available. Include enough information to identify the problem, such as the account email, approximate time, affected feature, and a short description. Do not send passwords, reset links, private keys, or other secrets in a support message.</p><p>These Terms intentionally use “applicable law” rather than inventing a specific governing-law clause or court location that has not been established in the actual Cookie service documents. The correct governing law, venue, arbitration rule, or mandatory consumer right can depend on the legal entity operating Cookie, the user's location, and other facts. Any final legal document used for contracting should be reviewed and approved by qualified counsel for the operator's jurisdiction.</p><p>If a clause is found unenforceable, the remaining clauses can continue to the extent permitted by law. A failure to enforce a clause in one situation does not automatically waive the ability to enforce it later. A user or Cookie administrator should not infer a permanent exception from a one-time support decision.</p><p>Cookie can publish updated Terms when the service or its legal obligations change. The effective date shown at the top of the current document is part of the version record. Where a change requires notice or consent under applicable law, Cookie should use an appropriate notice or consent mechanism rather than relying on silence alone.</p><p>For privacy-specific questions, use the <a href=\"#privacy\" data-legal-kind=\"privacy\">Privacy Policy</a> and its contact route. For technical issues, use support. For account enforcement, use the appeal or support path shown by Cookie. These channels keep operational questions separate from legal text while giving users a practical way to raise a problem.</p>"}];
const PRIVACY:LegalPageItem[]=[{"id":"p1","title":"Policy scope and data categories","summary":"What this Privacy Policy covers","bodyHtml":"<p>This <a href=\"#privacy\" data-legal-kind=\"privacy\">Privacy Policy</a> describes personal information that Cookie can process through its websites, web application, installed web experience, account system, AI features, workspace tools, sharing features, moderation controls, and related support flows. It focuses on information that is actually relevant to the current Cookie implementation and uses qualified language where a provider, feature, or account plan can change the exact data path.</p><p>Cookie can process different categories of information depending on what you do. Those categories can include account and contact details, authentication data, conversation content, uploaded files and images, project data, saved items, Memory data, sharing data, usage events, moderation records, technical request information, and communications such as account verification or password-reset emails. Not every category is collected from every user or every feature.</p><p>This Policy does not mean Cookie automatically reads every file on a device, records every action on a device, or collects information that the product never receives. A useful rule is simple: information you submit to Cookie can be processed; information generated by use of the service can create technical or account records; information held entirely outside Cookie is not magically visible to Cookie.</p><p>The operator should keep the legal identity and contact information associated with the production service current. This page is written so that it does not invent a company registration number, physical office, data-protection certification, or regulatory status. When a specific legal identity or regional representative is required for your situation, the current official Cookie contact information should be used.</p><p>Read this Policy together with the <a href=\"#terms\" data-legal-kind=\"terms\">Terms of Service</a>. The Terms explain acceptable use and account rules; this document explains personal-information processing. Both documents can be updated when Cookie changes.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Policy scope and data categories” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p2","title":"Account and profile information","summary":"Registration, profile data and account state","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie's account system can hold information such as an email address, name, username, avatar information, email-verification state, plan state, account role, credit balance, account status, and timestamps associated with account creation or updates. This information is needed to distinguish one account from another, provide account-level features, apply entitlements, and protect the service.</p><p>If you register with a password, Cookie's authentication system can store a password-derived value and a salt rather than the raw password. Email verification and password-reset flows can create short-lived token records so that the intended account holder can verify or recover access. These token records have an expiry and usage state; they are security records, not a permanent copy of the verification link.</p><p>Cookie can also store account status such as active, suspended, or banned when moderation or security action changes access. Authorized administrators can update plan, role, credits, and related account settings. Those administrative changes can be recorded in an audit log so that sensitive account-management activity is traceable.</p><p>You control the profile information you provide, but accuracy matters for support and account recovery. Do not use another person's email address, personal details, or identity without authorization. If the account contains the wrong information, use the profile or support mechanism Cookie provides to correct it.</p><p>Account information is not used for every possible purpose. Cookie should use it for functions connected to account administration, authentication, service delivery, security, support, moderation, and legal or operational needs described elsewhere in this Policy.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Account and profile information” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p3","title":"Authentication and security data","summary":"Sessions, verification and account protection","bodyHtml":"<p>When you sign in, Cookie can create a server-side session record and a secure session cookie in the browser. The session system associates a token hash with the account and has an expiration time. The browser does not need to store the server's raw token hash. This design helps the service identify an authenticated request while limiting the lifetime of a session.</p><p>Authentication flows can also create records for verification codes, secure password-reset tokens, failed sign-in attempts, and temporary account locks. These records exist because a sign-in service has to distinguish legitimate recovery from abuse. They are not general-purpose chat history and should not be assumed to be visible in the normal conversation interface.</p><p>Cookie can process request information that helps defend authentication and infrastructure, such as an IP address or forwarding header and the browser's user-agent. Admin audit records can include those values when a privileged account performs a sensitive operation. This information can help investigate account takeover, abuse, or unauthorized administrative access.</p><p>Cookie does not use an administrator-facing reset workflow that reveals a user's existing password. The administrator can request a secure password-reset email for an eligible account, after which the user follows the time-limited link. This separation reduces the need for administrators to handle password secrets.</p><p>Security records can be kept longer than ordinary chat content when necessary to investigate an incident, enforce access controls, document an administrative action, or comply with a legal obligation. Retention should be proportionate to the security purpose.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Authentication and security data” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p4","title":"Chats and conversation content","summary":"Prompts, replies and chat history","bodyHtml":"<p>When you use Cookie to send a message, the service receives the message content needed to generate a response. The current database structure stores chats and chat messages with account identifiers, timestamps, model information, titles, and conversation content. A normal saved conversation is therefore server-side application data, not only text that exists in your browser.</p><p>Cookie can process conversation content through its AI pipeline so the model can answer you. Depending on the request, relevant content can also be processed by supporting functions such as file handling, search, calculations, or other internal tools. The exact route can vary with the request and current service configuration.</p><p>Chat history can include user messages and assistant messages. Features such as pinning, archiving, deleting, branching, saving, and sharing can create additional state associated with the conversation. Deleting a chat from the visible history is a product action; it should not be described as an instantaneous erasure of every database, backup, moderation, or technical record.</p><p>Temporary chats can be designed not to remain in normal chat history, but temporary mode does not mean that the server never receives the content. Security, abuse-prevention, diagnostic, or legal records can still exist where legitimately needed. Users should avoid submitting secrets simply because a chat is marked temporary.</p><p>Because chat content can contain personal information supplied by the user, the safest practice is to share only what is necessary. Redaction, placeholders, and synthetic examples can often provide the same product benefit with less privacy exposure.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Chats and conversation content” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p5","title":"Files and image information","summary":"Uploads, metadata and processing","bodyHtml":"<p>When you attach a file or image, Cookie can receive the file bytes, the file name, MIME type, size, and related chat or project context needed to process the upload. Image uploads can be displayed in the interface and can be passed into an AI workflow when the feature supports image understanding. Documents can be converted or inspected as part of the requested task.</p><p>A file can contain personal information that is not obvious from its filename. For example, a document can include names, comments, hidden properties, revision history, embedded images, or metadata. Cookie's ability to process a file means that this information can become part of the service data associated with your request.</p><p>Where image generation is enabled, Cookie can create a generated image based on the request. Generated content is output from an AI system and can differ from the user's description. The generated file itself can become data that the user downloads, saves, or shares. Cookie cannot control copies made after the file leaves the service.</p><p>Uploaded files are subject to the same account and workspace controls as the feature where they were used. A project file can remain associated with a project until removed. A file referenced by a chat can remain associated with chat-related data. The exact deletion path should be based on the product control actually shown to the user.</p><p>Users should not upload information they do not have permission to process. In addition to privacy, this matters for confidentiality and intellectual-property rights. Cookie does not independently verify the permissions for each file.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Files and image information” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p6","title":"AI processing and service providers","summary":"How model requests are handled","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie uses AI model infrastructure to produce responses and, where enabled, images. A request can therefore be processed by the model system selected for that task. Cookie's current implementation can route different tasks to different model levels or specialized models. The exact model can change as Cookie updates, and the service should not be read as promising that one named model will always be used.</p><p>Relevant input can be sent to the AI provider or model runtime only as needed to generate the requested result. The service can also send limited context such as conversation history, file-derived text, or image data that the model needs to answer the request. The precise context included can depend on the feature and request.</p><p>Cookie currently uses Cloudflare infrastructure for the web application and functions, and Cloudflare Workers AI can provide model processing. Cookie also uses Neon-backed database storage for account and application data. Email delivery can use the configured SMTP path for verification, password reset, and release messages. These providers are part of the service's operational architecture.</p><p>Third-party processing does not mean Cookie sells your chat content. The service is described as using providers to operate the product, not as transferring ownership of your material to an infrastructure company. Provider-specific data practices can be governed by their own contracts and policies as applicable to the operator's relationship with them.</p><p>If Cookie adds or replaces a provider, this Policy can be updated to describe the new architecture. The goal is to describe actual data flows without inventing provider names or purposes that the product does not use.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “AI processing and service providers” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p7","title":"Memory","summary":"Saved memory and control","bodyHtml":"<p>When Memory is enabled, Cookie can keep selected information in a separate Memory record so future interactions can use that information. The current database model stores a memory key and value for each user and includes timestamps. Memory is therefore persistent application data rather than an informal promise that the model happens to remember something.</p><p>Memory is intended for useful continuity, such as preferences or recurring context. Users should not use it as a password manager or secure vault. A saved Memory item can be relevant to future AI requests, so information that would be inappropriate to disclose in a future conversation should not be saved there.</p><p>Cookie provides controls for reviewing and removing available Memory items. Deleting a Memory item removes that saved item from the Memory store, but it does not automatically delete every occurrence of the same information from chat messages, project files, exports, shared content, or operational logs where the information also exists.</p><p>Memory processing can change as Cookie's models and product design evolve. This Policy explains the current concept rather than promising a fixed internal algorithm. The important distinction is that Memory can be separately stored and separately controlled from the ordinary chat history.</p><p>If Memory is not enabled or a feature does not use Memory, Cookie should not claim that it is silently storing a memory from every message. Users can check the Memory controls available in the current product to understand whether the feature is on and which items are saved.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Memory” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p8","title":"Projects and saved workspace data","summary":"Organized work, files and saved items","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie can store project records, project descriptions, project files, saved messages, and other workspace items you explicitly create or save. The current data model associates these items with the account that created them. This makes it possible to reopen related work without keeping everything only in a local browser cache.</p><p>Project files can contain arbitrary text or code within the product's limits. A project can therefore become a significant source of personal or confidential information. The privacy risk is determined by what you put in the project, not simply by the word “project.” Store only what you are authorized to process and keep only what you still need.</p><p>Saved items can preserve a message, note, title, or URL for later use. These items are separate from the original chat state and can remain after the original message is viewed. Deleting a saved item should be understood as removing that saved workspace copy, not as proving that the same content no longer exists anywhere else.</p><p>Workspace features can be used for collaborative-style preparation, but a project should not be treated as an independent identity or permission system unless the product explicitly provides those controls. A user who shares a public link can disclose information that originated in a private workspace.</p><p>The product may evolve its project and workspace model. When a new control is introduced, the relevant interface should explain whether the control copies, moves, shares, or deletes the underlying content. This Policy avoids promising that those behaviors are identical across every feature.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Projects and saved workspace data” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p9","title":"Search and research data","summary":"Search requests, retrieved pages and citations","bodyHtml":"<p>When Cookie decides to search the web, the service can process the search query and retrieve public web information relevant to the request. The query can itself contain personal information if the user puts it there, so the user should avoid using identifiable details when the same research can be performed with a broader query.</p><p>Retrieved pages can contain third-party information that Cookie uses to generate a response. Cookie does not own those websites and does not control what they log when you later visit them. Once you follow a source link, the destination website's own privacy practices can apply.</p><p>Source links and citations can be stored as part of a response or chat history if the surrounding conversation is saved. A citation can therefore appear in the same workspace data as the answer that referenced it. The original source remains outside Cookie and may change or disappear later.</p><p>Cookie can use web search for current information but does not promise that every web result is complete, authoritative, or error-free. Research outputs can contain mistakes about what a source says. Users should open primary sources for important claims and should not treat a source link as a certification of accuracy.</p><p>The system can also fail to retrieve a source. A failed fetch should not be represented as proof that a page contains nothing. Where possible, Cookie should distinguish successful research from uncertainty or missing information.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Search and research data” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p10","title":"Usage and operational telemetry","summary":"How Cookie measures service operation","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie stores usage events in its application database. The current usage model can record an event kind, model identifier, unit count, user account, and creation time. These records help the service understand usage for account limits, credit accounting, model access, and operational reporting. The term “usage event” does not imply that Cookie records every keystroke or every action on the device.</p><p>Technical request data can also be processed while a request moves through Cloudflare infrastructure and Cookie's server-side functions. This can include normal network information, timestamps, request paths, and headers that are necessary for routing, authentication, security, or diagnostics. Cookie should not claim to collect a device signal merely because a web browser is capable of exposing it.</p><p>Performance and error information can be generated when something fails, for example when a model request times out or a function throws an exception. Logs and monitoring records are generally operational data rather than user-facing chat content. They can still contain identifiers or fragments needed to diagnose the issue, so the service should treat them as potentially sensitive.</p><p>Cookie does not promise that every operational record is visible to the user or that every record has the same retention period as a chat. Security and reliability data can need a different retention window. The service should retain only what is reasonably needed for the relevant operational purpose.</p><p>Users can reduce unnecessary collection by avoiding sensitive information in prompts and by using the minimum account and file data needed for the feature. The service's responsibility is to operate within the purposes described here rather than turning every technical field into an unrelated profiling system.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Usage and operational telemetry” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p11","title":"Cookies and local browser storage","summary":"Session cookies and local settings","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie uses browser storage for some client-side preferences and state. The current web application can use local storage to remember items such as selected model, effort setting, interface settings, library state, installation prompts, and a locally maintained chat cache or profile information. These values are stored in the user's browser and are separate from the server-side Neon records.</p><p>Authentication uses a secure session cookie so the server can recognize an authenticated browser. This cookie is a functional security mechanism. It is not the same as an advertising cookie and should not be represented as tracking the user's activity across unrelated websites.</p><p>Browser storage can be cleared by the user through device or browser controls. Clearing local storage can remove local preferences or cached state without necessarily deleting server-side chats, projects, or account data. Conversely, deleting server-side data does not automatically guarantee that a previously cached browser value has disappeared until the browser storage is cleared.</p><p>Cookie does not need to claim an elaborate advertising-cookie system when the current application does not implement one. The practical privacy issue for today's product is functional storage: session state, settings, installation state, and browser-local application data that help the interface work as expected.</p><p>Browser privacy modes, content blockers, or restricted storage settings can change product behavior. Cookie can therefore show a login error or lose a local preference even though the account database is functioning normally. Those are browser-configuration effects, not necessarily evidence of unauthorized data collection.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Cookies and local browser storage” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p12","title":"IP address and device information","summary":"Network, browser and request context","bodyHtml":"<p>A web service receives network information as part of ordinary HTTP requests. Cookie can process an IP address or a proxy-forwarded address for authentication, abuse prevention, security investigation, rate limiting, and administrative audit. This information can also appear in infrastructure logs maintained by Cloudflare or other providers operating the service.</p><p>Browser and user-agent information can be visible to server-side functions. Cookie can use it to understand whether a request came from a supported browser environment or to investigate a security event. The current code does not justify claiming that the service collects a permanent hardware identifier such as a device serial number.</p><p>Approximate location can sometimes be inferred from an IP address by the service provider or network operator. Cookie should not describe this as a precise GPS location unless the product actually asks the device for location permission and receives it. The ordinary web architecture described here is not a hidden GPS tracker.</p><p>Network information can be included in privileged admin audit records when administrators perform sensitive account actions. That audit data is intentionally restricted to higher-level administrative roles. The purpose is accountability: when an account is reset, credits are changed, or a security-sensitive setting is changed, the operator should be able to identify who performed the action and from which request context.</p><p>Because IP addresses and user-agent strings can be personal information in some jurisdictions, Cookie should handle them according to the purposes and retention rules described in this Policy and applicable law. Users should not infer from a normal web request that Cookie knows their exact physical location.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “IP address and device information” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p13","title":"Moderation and audit records","summary":"Safety, enforcement and administrative accountability","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie maintains moderation records so that warnings, suspensions, bans, restorations, and related actions can be understood and enforced consistently. The current moderation event model can include the target user, the administrator or moderator who acted, the action, a reason, a duration where relevant, and a timestamp. Users can receive moderation notifications derived from these records.</p><p>Higher-level account administration can also create a separate audit record. The current admin audit model can record the actor account, actor email and role, target account when there is one, the action, success or failure, request IP, user-agent, metadata, and creation time. Access to the audit view is restricted to owner and admin roles in the current product.</p><p>Audit records exist because privileged actions can have serious consequences. An administrator who changes a user's plan, credits, role, password-reset flow, limits, or moderation state should not be able to do so without an accountable record. These records are not the same thing as general user activity tracking and should not be used as a reason to claim that Cookie records every action a person takes everywhere on the internet.</p><p>Moderation information can be retained separately from chat history because an account restriction may need to remain explainable after a chat is deleted. That does not mean every moderation note is public. Users should see the account-level notice and the information Cookie chooses to disclose through the notification system, while internal security details can remain restricted.</p><p>Cookie can also log the act of viewing sensitive administrative information. This creates a second level of accountability: not only can an administrative action be recorded, but access to the audit system itself can be recorded. The goal is controlled administration, not general surveillance of users.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Moderation and audit records” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p14","title":"Email communications","summary":"Verification, password reset and product announcements","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie can send transactional email to an account address for verification and password recovery. The verification flow can include a one-time code or link that expires. The password-reset flow can include a secure, time-limited tokenized link. These messages are necessary for account security and are different from optional marketing messages.</p><p>Cookie can also send product release announcements to registered account email addresses when an owner-authorized release announcement is triggered. The current implementation is designed for deliberate, release-level broadcasts rather than an email on every code commit. The email can include new features, improvements, and a link back to Cookie.</p><p>Email delivery can use the configured SMTP infrastructure. The service may process the recipient address, message metadata, and the content of the email needed to deliver it. Do not assume that email is private from the mail provider; normal email infrastructure necessarily involves the provider that transmits the message.</p><p>A password-reset email should never contain the existing password. It instead gives the account owner a secure path to choose a new password. If you receive a reset email you did not request, do not follow the link unless you recognize the request; changing your password through the official Cookie flow is safer than sending the link to support.</p><p>Transactional security emails can continue even when a user's chat activity is paused because authentication and recovery are separate account functions. Users can ignore an unexpected verification or release email, but should report suspected account takeover through support.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Email communications” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p15","title":"Sharing and external disclosure","summary":"Public links, recipients and onward copies","bodyHtml":"<p>When a user creates a shared chat or otherwise publishes content through Cookie, the data included in that share can become accessible to recipients of the link or publication mechanism. The current service has a shared-chat concept that stores a payload for the published view. The privacy effect comes from the disclosure itself: content that was once accessible only to the account can become accessible to other people.</p><p>Cookie cannot control what a recipient does after seeing a shared item. A recipient can copy text, download content, take screenshots, or forward the link. Removing the share can stop the normal Cookie access path where that control exists, but it cannot recall a copy already made by another person.</p><p>External disclosure can also happen when a user intentionally downloads a file and sends it to another service, follows a third-party source link, or uses an integration outside Cookie. Once information leaves Cookie, the receiving service's privacy rules apply. Cookie cannot promise that an external destination will use the same retention or security practices.</p><p>Users should check a shared conversation for names, email addresses, account details, private messages, source files, and hidden context before publishing. A generated summary can still contain private facts from the original conversation even when it looks shorter than the source.</p><p>Cookie's responsibility is to provide the sharing mechanism and the controls it actually implements, not to pretend that a share link is a sealed document. The user makes the publication decision, and the <a href=\"#terms\" data-legal-kind=\"terms\">Terms of Service</a> govern acceptable use of the resulting disclosure.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Sharing and external disclosure” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p16","title":"Retention, deletion and backups","summary":"How long information can remain","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie can retain different information for different periods because account operation, security, moderation, usage accounting, and chat history do not have the same operational needs. A chat may have one lifecycle, a session token another, a moderation record another, and an audit log another. The current application does not establish a single universal number of days for every data category.</p><p>Deleting a visible record can remove it from the normal user interface while technical, security, backup, or audit copies can persist for some additional period. For example, a moderation record may need to remain to explain an enforcement action, and a session record may need to remain briefly to expire or revoke authentication state. These are different purposes from keeping a conversation available in chat history.</p><p>Where Cookie provides a delete control, use that control for the type of data it describes. Do not infer that “delete” always means cryptographic destruction from every backup, cache, provider log, and lawful record. Complete deletion can also require propagation through external systems, which may take time.</p><p>Cookie can keep information when necessary for security, abuse prevention, dispute handling, fraud investigation, service integrity, or legal requirements. The service should not use those reasons as a blanket license to keep unrelated data forever. Retention should remain connected to a legitimate operational need.</p><p>When an account is closed, the operator should distinguish between account deactivation, visible content deletion, and eventual removal from systems that are allowed to retain a limited record. Users with a specific deletion question should use the privacy or support contact so the request can be evaluated against the actual data category involved.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Retention, deletion and backups” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p17","title":"Privacy choices and requests","summary":"Access, correction, deletion and control","bodyHtml":"<p>Depending on the feature and the law that applies to you, you may be able to control information through Cookie's own settings, chat controls, Memory controls, sharing controls, or account-support processes. These product controls are the first place to look because they can make a change immediately without waiting for manual handling.</p><p>A privacy request can include questions about the information Cookie holds, a correction request, a deletion request, or a request to stop a particular use where that right applies. The operator may need to verify that the requester is the account owner before disclosing or changing account information. Verification should be proportionate and should not require a user to send a password or a secret reset token.</p><p>Not every request can necessarily be fulfilled in the same way. Some records can be retained for security, moderation, legal compliance, dispute handling, or other legitimate purposes. Applicable law can also recognize exceptions. Cookie should explain the outcome rather than claiming that every deletion request automatically erases every record regardless of context.</p><p>The current product includes practical controls for deleting chats, managing Memory, changing account settings, and controlling shares where those controls are implemented. Those controls are not a replacement for every legal privacy right, and they are not a promise that a product button can change data held by a separate provider.</p><p>When contacting Cookie about privacy, describe the account and the request without including unnecessary sensitive information. Use the privacy or support contact published by the actual service. The contact details should remain current in production rather than being invented in a template.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Privacy choices and requests” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p18","title":"Children, international processing and legal disclosures","summary":"Age, geography and required disclosures","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie is intended to be used only by people who are legally permitted to use the service. The current account flow does not justify claiming that Cookie has a universal age-verification system. Where a parent, guardian, school, employer, or local law imposes additional requirements, those requirements can limit how the service should be used.</p><p>Information submitted to Cookie can be processed in countries other than the user's home country because cloud infrastructure, AI processing, database services, email systems, and other providers can operate internationally. The service should therefore avoid promising that all data remains in one specific country unless that location is actually guaranteed for the relevant product tier and provider configuration.</p><p>Cookie can disclose information when required by applicable law, valid legal process, or a legitimate governmental demand. It can also disclose information when reasonably necessary to protect users, prevent serious abuse or fraud, investigate security incidents, enforce service rules, or protect the operator's rights. Such disclosure should remain tied to a lawful and legitimate purpose rather than a general interest in users' lives.</p><p>International processing does not automatically mean that every provider can use data for its own unrelated purposes. Cookie's role is to configure service providers for the functions they perform and to describe those functions honestly. Provider-specific legal arrangements can also add requirements not visible in the user interface.</p><p>If a child or another ineligible person has created an account and a parent or guardian believes information was provided improperly, the appropriate step is to contact the service through the published privacy or support route. The operator can then determine what action is appropriate under the applicable rules and the actual account state.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Children, international processing and legal disclosures” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p19","title":"Security and incident response","summary":"Protecting accounts, investigating incidents and handling security records","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie can update this <a href=\"#privacy\" data-legal-kind=\"privacy\">Privacy Policy</a> when the service changes, when the data architecture changes, or when legal requirements change. The effective date at the top identifies the version intended to apply from that date. Users should check the current Policy from the Cookie interface rather than relying on an old copied page from another source.</p><p>When a change is material and a notice or consent is required by applicable law, Cookie should use an appropriate notice or consent method. Not every technical change needs a new consent prompt. For example, changing an internal implementation detail that preserves the same data purpose can be documented without pretending that a new privacy contract was created.</p><p>For privacy questions, use the privacy or support contact published by Cookie. A useful request can identify the account email, the data category involved, and the action requested. Do not send passwords, verification codes, reset tokens, or other authentication secrets in a privacy request.</p><p>Cookie does not use this page to invent an office address, data-protection officer, registration number, certification, or jurisdiction-specific legal claim that has not been confirmed for the production service. Those details should be added only when the operator has verified them.</p><p>This Policy is meant to be understandable as a real product privacy notice. It describes the current account, AI, workspace, sharing, moderation, security, email, and infrastructure flows without claiming that Cookie has capabilities or legal statuses that are not actually implemented.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Security and incident response” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"},{"id":"p20","title":"Changes and contact","summary":"Policy updates and how to reach Cookie","bodyHtml":"<p>Cookie can update this <a href=\"#privacy\" data-legal-kind=\"privacy\">Privacy Policy</a> when the service changes, when the data architecture changes, or when legal requirements change. The effective date at the top identifies the version intended to apply from that date. Users should check the current Policy from the Cookie interface rather than relying on an old copied page from another source.</p><p>When a change is material and a notice or consent is required by applicable law, Cookie should use an appropriate notice or consent method. Not every technical change needs a new consent prompt. For example, changing an internal implementation detail that preserves the same data purpose can be documented without pretending that a new privacy contract was created.</p><p>For privacy questions, use the privacy or support contact published by Cookie. A useful request can identify the account email, the data category involved, and the action requested. Do not send passwords, verification codes, reset tokens, or other authentication secrets in a privacy request.</p><p>Cookie does not use this page to invent an office address, data-protection officer, registration number, certification, or jurisdiction-specific legal claim that has not been confirmed for the production service. Those details should be added only when the operator has verified them.</p><p>This Policy is meant to be understandable as a real product privacy notice. It describes the current account, AI, workspace, sharing, moderation, security, email, and infrastructure flows without claiming that Cookie has capabilities or legal statuses that are not actually implemented.</p><h3>How this applies in Cookie</h3><p>The privacy rule for “Changes and contact” is limited to the information that Cookie actually receives, creates, stores, or sends as part of the feature. The service should not be understood as having hidden access to unrelated device content merely because a browser or operating system could technically expose it. At the same time, information placed into a prompt, upload, project, Memory item, account profile, or shared link can become part of the service data for that feature and should be treated accordingly.</p><p>A useful privacy check is to ask three questions before using a feature: what information am I sending, why does Cookie need it for this task, and who else may process it because the feature requires a provider or an intentional share? Use the least sensitive version of the information that still solves the task, use the product's deletion or control options when appropriate, and contact the privacy or support route for a request that cannot be handled through the interface. Those steps reduce ambiguity without creating promises about data that Cookie cannot actually control.</p>"}];

function LegalLink({kind,label,onNavigate}:{kind:Kind;label:string;onNavigate:(kind:Kind)=>void}){
 return <button type="button" className="legal-inline-link" onClick={()=>onNavigate(kind)}>{label}</button>;
}

export default function LegalPage({kind,onBack}:{kind:Kind;onBack:()=>void}){
 const privacy=kind==="privacy";
 const pages=privacy?PRIVACY:TERMS;
 const [pageId,setPageId]=useState(pages[0].id);
 useEffect(()=>{setPageId(pages[0].id)},[kind]);
 const index=Math.max(0,pages.findIndex(p=>p.id===pageId));
 const page=pages[index]||pages[0];
 const related=useMemo(()=>privacy?"Read the Terms of Service for the rules that govern accounts, use, moderation, plans and the service.":"Read the Privacy Policy for the information Cookie processes, why it is processed, sharing, retention and privacy controls.",[privacy]);
 const go=(next:number)=>{const target=pages[next];if(target){setPageId(target.id);window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:"smooth"})}};
 const onNavigate=(next:Kind)=>{if(next===kind){setPageId(pages[0].id);window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:"smooth"})}else{window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("cookie:open-legal",{detail:next}))}};
 const onHtmlClick=(event:React.MouseEvent<HTMLDivElement>)=>{const el=(event.target as HTMLElement)?.closest?.("[data-legal-kind]") as HTMLElement|null;const next=el?.getAttribute("data-legal-kind") as Kind|null;if(next){event.preventDefault();onNavigate(next)}};
 return <div className="legal-page">
  <header className="legal-head">
   <button onClick={onBack} className="legal-back"><ArrowLeft size={17}/>Help Center</button>
   <div className="legal-head-title"><b>Cookie</b><span>{privacy?"Privacy Policy":"Terms of Service"}</span></div>
  </header>
  <div className="legal-layout">
   <aside className="legal-nav">
    <div className="legal-nav-top"><span className="legal-kicker">{privacy?<LockKeyhole size={15}/>:<ShieldCheck size={15}/>}COOKIE</span><h2>{privacy?"Privacy Policy":"Terms of Service"}</h2><p>20 topics</p></div>
    <nav aria-label={privacy?"Privacy Policy topics":"Terms of Service topics"}>{pages.map((p,i)=><button key={p.id} className={p.id===page.id?"selected":""} onClick={()=>go(i)}><span>{String(i+1).padStart(2,"0")}</span>{p.title}</button>)}</nav>
   </aside>
   <main className="legal-document">
    <div className="legal-document-kicker">{privacy?<LockKeyhole size={15}/>:<FileText size={15}/>} {privacy?"PRIVACY":"TERMS"}</div>
    <h1>{page.title}</h1>
    <p className="legal-updated">Cookie {privacy?"Privacy Policy":"Terms of Service"} · Effective October 6, 2026 · Topic {index+1} of 20</p>
    <div className="legal-copy">
      <p className="legal-lead">{page.summary}</p>
      <div className="legal-html" onClick={onHtmlClick} dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{__html:page.bodyHtml}} />
      <div className="legal-related"><b>Related document</b><p>{related} <LegalLink kind={privacy?"terms":"privacy"} label={privacy?"Terms of Service":"Privacy Policy"} onNavigate={onNavigate} />.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div className="legal-pager">
      <button disabled={index===0} onClick={()=>go(index-1)}><ChevronLeft size={17}/><span><small>Previous</small>{index>0?pages[index-1].title:"Start of document"}</span></button>
      <button disabled={index===pages.length-1} onClick={()=>go(index+1)}><span><small>Next</small>{index<pages.length-1?pages[index+1].title:"End of document"}</span><ChevronRight size={17}/></button>
    </div>
    <div className="legal-notice"><b>Legal drafting note</b><p>This page is written around Cookie's current product behavior and avoids asserting an unverified corporate identity, jurisdiction, certification, payment rule, age-gate, deletion period, or regulatory status. It is a production-facing service draft, not legal advice; the operator should have qualified counsel review the final text for the actual contracting entity, jurisdiction and mandatory consumer/privacy requirements.</p></div>
    <footer className="legal-foot"><span>Cookie</span><nav><LegalLink kind="terms" label="Terms of Service" onNavigate={onNavigate}/><LegalLink kind="privacy" label="Privacy Policy" onNavigate={onNavigate}/></nav></footer>
   </main>
  </div>
 </div>;
}
